  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday, an upper low will move into central-southern California\nwhile a downstream shortwave ridge exits southeastern Arizona to\ngive way to southwesterly flow. Satellite derived moisture\nvalues aren't terribly impressive this morning with\nprecipitable water at 0.8-1 inches, however with the trough\nnearby a slight chance of a shower or thunderstorm is in the \nforecast this afternoon mainly east of central Pinal/Pima \nthrough the New Mexico border. Moisture is expected to advect \nnorth out of Mexico tonight as low-mid level flow turns \nsoutherly, which will keep a slight chance of a shower in the \nforecast through the night. \n\nTuesday through Thursday will see upper level troughing persist\nover southern California and northern Baja, while a southerly \nstream of moisture continues, mainly focused over New Mexico \nwith central Pima on the western periphery. Precipitable water \nshould increase to 1-1.25 inches in this area and combined with\npersistent upper level support, shower/thunderstorm chances \nwill increase to 30-60 percent in eastern areas of the state.. \n\nPrecipitation chances lower a bit Friday into the weekend as the\nupper level wave finally exits the region. Confidence in any one\nforecast solution in this period going forward deteriorates\nas ensemble models disagree on the flow pattern as well as the \npotential influence of Tropical Storm Polo currently off the \nsouthwestern Mexico coast. At this moment the forecast favors \njust low end rain chances through early next week with most \nensemble members keeping tropical activity away from the region \nthrough mid-week next week.",
